Atlas System DAO-Inspired Governance Framework

1. Purpose of the Document

This document describes the governance approach of Atlas: Atlas is not positioned as a full DAO, but it may use selected mechanics inspired by DAOs for voting, community participation, and discussion of the ecosystem’s development.

2. Why Not a “Full DAO”

Atlas must not promise full decentralization of governance if:

key smart contract parameters are not fully governed by a DAO;

the team retains a role in the development of the interface, security, and materials;

not all decisions are put to a vote;

governance mechanics are introduced gradually;

the legal, technical, or security part remains with responsible members of the team.

3. What DAO-Inspired Mechanics May Cover

DAO-inspired mechanics may be used for:

voting on educational initiatives;

selecting webinar topics;

evaluating community proposals;

prioritizing the roadmap;

discussing interface improvements;

community polls;

security proposals;

feedback on materials;

the participation of active statuses in the development of the ecosystem.

4. What Community Governance May Decide

Community governance may discuss and vote on:

interface changes;

roadmap priorities;

educational initiatives;

webinar rules;

the parameters of public materials;

security proposals;

the development of the partner program, provided this does not violate already active rules.

5. What Governance Must Not Do

Governance must not:

promise payouts;

override the Risk Disclosure;

force participants into actions;

change private keys or access credentials;

guarantee liquidity;

replace the smart contract with a manual decision.

6. Who May Participate

The right to participate in particular votes may depend on:

the connected wallet;

the participant’s status;

activity in the system;

participation history;

the role in the community;

the rules of the specific vote.

7. Vote Weight

Vote weight is the weight of a participant’s vote in a specific vote. It may be the same for everyone or may depend on status, activity, reputation, governance tokens, NFTs, the participation period, or other published criteria.

If vote weight is used, it must be described in advance, before the vote begins.

8. Proposal Lifecycle

The standard lifecycle of a proposal may include:

An idea or proposal draft.

Preliminary discussion.

Publication of the final text.

The voting period.

Tallying the result.

Publication of the decision.

Execution, if the decision falls within an area that governance can actually change.

9. Quorum and Timeframes

A vote is considered informational if quorum is not reached. If quorum is reached, the decision may be taken under consideration by the team or executed within the previously described scope of governance.

10. Informational and Binding Votes

Not all votes are required to carry the same force.

Atlas may have:

informational community polls;

advisory votes, which help the team make a decision;

binding votes, if a specific area has been delegated to a governance mechanic in advance;

test votes at an early stage.

11. Connection with the Partner Program and Webinars

DAO-inspired mechanics may be used to select topics for international webinars, evaluate speakers, and gather proposals on materials, languages, scheduling, and community initiatives.

This does not mean that participants may, through a vote, reassign other people’s referrals, change attribution, override the rules of the partner program, or appropriate another person’s structure.

12. Hard Limitations

Governance cannot be a tool for bypassing the Risk Disclosure, promising payouts, pressuring participants, or changing financial expectations retroactively. Any DAO-inspired mechanics must strengthen transparency, not create the illusion of guaranteed profit or full collective control over the smart contract.

13. Acceptance of the Rules

By participating in a vote, proposal, poll, or community governance activity of Atlas, the participant confirms that they understand: Atlas uses selected DAO-inspired mechanics, but is not a full DAO, unless this is expressly published in a separate final version of the governance document.

Atlas System Interface Terms of Use

1. Purpose of the Document

These terms describe the rules for using the website, personal account, pages, forms, widgets, reference materials, webinars, and other interfaces of the Atlas System.

The Atlas System interface is intended to let the participant see the system’s possibilities in an understandable form: available Smart Cycles, calculated parameters, statuses, partner metrics, webinars, materials, action history, and available on-chain operations.

The interface does not replace the Smart Cycle rules, the risk disclosure, or the actual logic of the smart contract. If the interface displays calculated possibilities, they must be understood together with the Smart Cycle Rules and the Risk Disclosure.

If the participant does not agree with these terms, they must not use the Atlas interface.

2. Role of the Interface

The Atlas System interface is an auxiliary Web3 tool. It helps the participant see the available actions and build a transaction, which the participant independently verifies and confirms in their own wallet.

Through the interface, the participant can see:

available Smart Cycle formats;

the cycle term;

the cycle amount;

the calculated delta;

an example of a potential result;

the status of an active cycle;

the availability of the “Request assistance” action;

the partner structure and status;

educational materials and webinars;

security tips and official links.

The interface is not:

a bank;

a deposit platform;

an investment service;

a broker;

an asset management company;

a payment operator;

a custodial wallet;

a guarantor of request fulfillment;

a party that takes funds under management.

3. Connecting a Wallet

To use some of the functions, the participant may connect an external Web3 wallet, for example MetaMask or another compatible wallet.

Connecting a wallet does not mean transferring control of the wallet to Atlas System. The participant independently manages their wallet, confirms or rejects transactions, pays network fees, and is responsible for the security of access.

Atlas System does not request or store the seed phrase, private key, wallet password, or recovery codes.

4. What the Participant Sees Before an Action

Before creating a Smart Cycle, the interface may show the participant a calculation screen:

the selected cycle format;

the term;

the amount;

the calculated delta;

an example of a potential result;

the network fee or the need for gas;

a warning that the result is not guaranteed;

a link to the Smart Cycle Rules and the Risk Disclosure.

5. Signing Transactions

Any on-chain action is performed only after the participant confirms it in their wallet. Before confirming, the participant must verify:

the website address;

the network;

the smart contract address;

the type of action;

the amount;

the network fee;

token approvals, if they are required;

that the transaction is clear and expected.

If the participant does not understand the transaction, they must not sign it.

6. No Custody of Funds

The Atlas System interface does not store the participant’s funds and does not hold the private key. Funds and actions related to a Smart Cycle are processed according to the rules of the smart contract and the blockchain network.

Atlas System cannot manually cancel, reverse, change, or overwrite a transaction unless such a possibility is provided for by the smart contract.

7. Data Display

The interface may display data from the blockchain, APIs, indexers, RPC providers, local cache, analytics modules, and internal reference sources.

The displayed data may temporarily differ from the actual state of the blockchain due to delays, RPC errors, indexing problems, caching, page refreshes, or network failures.

The state of the blockchain and the logic of the smart contract take priority. The participant must independently verify critically important data before taking action.

8. Personal Account, Webinars, and Materials

The Atlas System personal account may be used not only for on-chain actions, but also to engage the participant in the system:

viewing educational materials;

registering for webinars;

submitting an application to host a webinar, if the participant’s status allows it;

viewing partner statistics;

obtaining official links;

studying Smart Cycle calculation tables;

understanding one’s status and next steps.

9. Availability of the Interface

The interface is provided “as is” and “as available.” Atlas System does not guarantee the constant availability of the website, personal account, API, analytics, FAQ sections, materials, webinars, or support.

Technical maintenance, updates, failures, blocks, overloads, hosting errors, network problems, attacks, access restrictions in certain regions, and other circumstances are possible.

10. Prohibited Use

The user must not use the Atlas System interface for:

hacking, exploiting vulnerabilities, or bypassing restrictions;

attacks on the website, API, smart contract, infrastructure, or users;

mass automated spam;

spoofing the interface or phishing;

distributing malicious links;

fraud, deception, or misleading participants;

promises of guaranteed income on behalf of Atlas System;

violating applicable laws;

actions that harm the system, participants, partners, or the reputation of Atlas System.

Atlas System may restrict access to the interface, hide materials, disable functions, block suspicious activity, or pass information to technical and legal specialists if this is necessary for security.

11. Official Links and Security

The participant must use only official Atlas System domains and official links. Before connecting a wallet, the website address must be verified.

Atlas System never asks for the seed phrase, private key, remote access to the device, or the transfer of funds to a support member’s personal wallet.

If the participant receives a suspicious link, message, form, or request to sign an unclear transaction, they must stop the action and verify the information through an official channel.

12. Materials and Content

The interface may contain texts, FAQs, presentations, videos, webinars, tables, tips, calculations, images, analytical data, and other materials.

Such materials may show the mechanics of Atlas System, calculation examples, the career system, the partner program, and the financial possibilities of a Smart Cycle. They are informational in nature and are not individual financial advice, investment advice, a legal opinion, tax advice, or a guarantee of a result.

If a material contradicts the Smart Cycle Rules, the Risk Disclosure, the published terms, or the actual logic of the smart contract, the official rules and the smart contract take priority.

13. Referral and Partner Links

The interface may support referral links, statuses, partner metrics, and educational materials.

Partner functions are intended for distributing information and educating participants. They may show career status, structure, matching bonus, partner percentage, access to webinar applications, and other program features. They must not be used for promises of guaranteed profit, pressure, manipulation, false statements, or concealment of risks.

A participant who distributes an Atlas System link is required to explain the risks and use correct wording.

14. Limitation of Liability

To the maximum extent permitted by applicable rules, Atlas System is not liable for:

loss of access to the wallet;

disclosure of the seed phrase or private key;

the participant’s actions in the wallet;

errors of the network, RPC, wallet, or device;

phishing and fake websites;

incorrectly signed transactions;

the absence of liquidity;

delays or the impossibility of fulfilling requests;

the actions of third-party partners, leaders, or speakers;

changes in legislation or access restrictions;

losses arising from the use of the interface.

15. Updates to the Terms

Atlas System may update the interface, functions, materials, display parameters, and these terms. Updates may be related to security, product development, the smart contract, DAO decisions, infrastructure, or legal requirements.

By continuing to use the interface after an update, the participant confirms their agreement with the current version of the terms.

16. Acceptance of the Terms

By using the Atla Systems interface, connecting a wallet, viewing data, creating a Smart Cycle, submitting an assistance request, or interacting with materials, the participant confirms that:

they have read these terms;

they understand the role of the interface as an auxiliary Web3 tool;

they see the calculated possibilities of the system as parameters, not as a guarantee;

they independently verify transactions;

they accept the risks of Web3 interaction;

they will not use the interface to harm the system or other participants.

Atlas System Privacy Policy

1. Purpose of the Document

This policy describes which data may be processed when using the website, personal account, Web3 interface, analytics sections, forms, materials, webinars, and support of the Atlas System.

Atlas System strives for data minimization. For basic Web3 interaction, the participant does not need to provide a passport, private key, seed phrase, or access to their wallet.

2.What Kind of Data Does Atlas System Not Request

Atlas System does not request:

the seed phrase;

the private key;

the wallet password;

recovery codes;

remote access to the device;

passport data for a basic wallet connection;

bank logins or passwords.

If anyone requests this data on behalf of Atlas System, it should be considered a suspicious action or an attempted fraud.

3. Public Wallet Address

When a Web3 wallet is connected, the interface may see the participant’s public wallet address.

The public wallet address is not a private key and, on its own, does not give Atlas System access to the participant’s funds. However, the public address may be linked to on-chain activity, transactions, Smart Cycles, referral structure, statuses, votes, or other actions in the system.

The participant must understand that blockchain data is, by its nature, public and may be accessible not only to Atlas System.

4. On-Chain Data

Transactions, addresses, smart contract events, amounts, statuses, timestamps, and other blockchain data may be publicly available in blockchain explorers and other services.

Atlas System cannot delete, hide, or change data already recorded in the public blockchain if such a possibility does not exist in the network or the smart contract itself.

5. Technical Data

When using the interface, technical data necessary for operation, security, and service improvement may be processed:

IP address;

date and time of access;

device type;

browser;

interface language;

country or region determined technically;

page URL;

referral parameters;

interface errors;

session data;

server logs;

performance data.

Such data may be processed by hosting, CDN, RPC providers, analytics services, and protection, logging, or monitoring systems.

6. Referral and Partner Data

Atlas System may process referral links, invite parameters, the structure of invitations, statuses, levels, partner metrics, and related technical records.

This data is needed to display the partner program, statistics, statuses, structure, and the correct retention of attribution: who invited the participant and through which link they arrived.

7. Support and Communication Data

If the participant contacts support, fills out a form, takes part in a webinar, submits an application, asks a question, or writes in an official channel, Atlas System may process the data the participant has provided themselves:

name or pseudonym;

Telegram/Email/contact;

wallet address;

language;

country or time zone;

the text of the request;

screenshots;

error information;

communication history.

The seed phrase, private key, passwords, or other secret data must not be sent to support.

8. Webinars and Educational Activities

When registering for a webinar or submitting an application to host a webinar, Atlas System may process:

name or pseudonym;

contact for communication;

webinar language;

country and time zone;

participant status;

the expected audience;

the webinar topic;

questions and comments;

the fact of participation or registration.

If a webinar is recorded, participants may be informed of the recording in advance. The speaker and participants must not disclose a private key, seed phrase, or confidential data during the broadcast.

9. Cookies and Local Storage

The interface may use cookies, local storage, or similar technologies for:

saving interface settings;

language;

the state of tabs;

drafts;

authorization or connection;

analytics;

security;

the prevention of abuse.

If certain cookies or analytics tools are not used, Atlas System may update this policy and explicitly specify the actual set of technologies.

10. Purposes of Processing

Data may be used for:

the operation of the Web3 interface;

displaying Smart Cycles and statuses;

retaining referral attribution;

user support;

conducting webinars;

security and anti-fraud;

error diagnostics;

product analytics;

improving the interface;

meeting technical and legal requirements.

11. Transfer to Third Parties

Atlas System may use external infrastructure: hosting, CDN, RPC, blockchain explorers, analytics services, logging services, email/messenger tools, webinar platforms, and protection systems.

Such providers may process technical data within the scope of their role. Atlas System does not sell the seed phrase or private key, because it does not request or store them.

12. International Nature

Atlas System is an international Web3 project. Data, infrastructure, participants, providers, and blockchain networks may be located in different countries.

By using Atlas System, the participant understands that the technical processing and availability of data may have a cross-border nature.

13. Retention Period

The retention period depends on the type of data:

on-chain data may be stored on the blockchain indefinitely;

technical logs may be stored for a limited period for security and diagnostics;

support requests may be stored until the issue is resolved and for a reasonable period afterward;

referral and status data may be stored as long as it is needed for the operation of the system;

local data in the browser may be stored until the user clears it.

14. User Rights and Requests

The participant may contact the official support channel regarding data that is under the control of Atlas System.

At the same time, Atlas System cannot delete or change public blockchain records, the data of third-party services, local data on the participant’s device, or information that the participant has disclosed to third parties themselves.

15. Security

Atlas System applies reasonable technical and organizational measures to protect the interface and related data. However, no Web3 interface, website, server, wallet, or network can be absolutely secure.

The participant is responsible for protecting their own device, wallet, seed phrase, private key, browser, extensions, and account access.

16. Updates to the Policy

Atlas System may update this policy when the interface, infrastructure, analytics, support, webinars, partner program, or legal requirements change.

The current version of the policy should be available in the interface or in the official materials of Atlas System.

17. Acceptance of the Policy

By using the Atlas System interface, connecting a wallet, registering for a webinar, submitting an application, or contacting support, the participant confirms that they have read this policy and understand the nature of data processing in a Web3 environment.

Atlas System Risk Disclosure

1. Purpose of the Document

This risk disclosure describes the main risks associated with using the Atlas System, connecting a Web3 wallet, creating a Smart Cycle, and interacting with the smart contract.

This document is not individual advice, a financial recommendation, investment advice, a legal opinion, or a guarantee of any result. The participant must independently assess whether participation in Atlas is suitable for them and, if necessary, obtain independent advice.

If the participant does not understand even one of the risks described here, they should not connect a wallet, create a Smart Cycle, or confirm transactions.

2. General Nature of Participation

The Atlas System is not a bank, a deposit platform, an investment fund, a broker, an asset management company, a payment institution, or a guaranteed-income service.

Participation in Atlas is voluntary. The participant alone decides whether to connect a wallet, which Smart Cycle to choose, the amount, the term, and whether to confirm the transaction. All actions are taken by the participant at their own risk.

3. No Guarantee of Return

Atlas does not guarantee the return of the cycle amount, receipt of the calculated delta, the timeframe for fulfilling an assistance request, or any financial result.

The calculated delta is a parameter of the Smart Cycle mechanics and must not be perceived as guaranteed profit, deposit interest, investment income, a fixed obligation, or a promised payout.

The participant must assume that they may not receive the expected result, may receive a result later than the expected term, or may face the impossibility of fulfilling a request due to a liquidity shortage or other conditions of the protocol.

4. Liquidity Risk

The fulfillment of assistance requests depends on the available liquidity in the smart contract and the rules of the protocol.

If liquidity is insufficient, a participant’s request may:

not be fulfilled immediately;

wait until available liquidity appears;

be partially fulfilled, if such logic is provided for;

be processed according to the rules of order or other rules of the smart contract;

not lead to the result the participant expects.

Available liquidity may change due to participant activity, inflows and outflows, network conditions, technical factors, and other circumstances.

5. Smart Contract Risk

Atlas uses smart-contract mechanics. Smart contracts may contain errors, vulnerabilities, logic limitations, incompatibilities, unforeseen behavior, or may depend on external infrastructure.

Even if the code is reviewed by the team or by third-party specialists, this does not eliminate all possible errors. An audit, testing, or public verifiability of the code is not a guarantee of security and does not remove all risks.

Interaction with the smart contract may be irreversible. If the participant has confirmed a transaction in their wallet, it may be impossible to cancel it manually.

6. Network, Gas, and RPC Risk

Web3 interaction depends on the blockchain network, fees, RPC providers, the wallet, the browser, and the participant’s device.

The following may occur:

high or unstable network fees;

delays in transaction confirmation;

RPC errors;

temporary network unavailability;

failures in displaying statuses in the interface;

discrepancies between the interface display and the state of the blockchain;

failed transactions with loss of the gas fee.

The participant bears the cost of network fees themselves and must verify the transaction details before confirming.

7. User Error Risk

The participant is responsible for their own actions and settings.

User errors include, among others:

connecting to a fake website;

selecting the wrong network;

sending funds to the wrong address;

confirming an unclear transaction;

an incorrect amount;

using an infected device;

losing access to the wallet;

disclosing the seed phrase or private key;

following a phishing link;

trusting unofficial support or unofficial channels.

Atlas cannot recover the seed phrase, private key, or access to the participant’s wallet.

8. Phishing and Fake Support Risk

Fake websites, channels, support accounts, bots, forms, and links may appear around Web3 projects.

Atlas never asks for:

the seed phrase;

the private key;

remote access to the device;

transferring funds to a manager’s personal wallet;

signing a transaction without a clear purpose.

Before any action, the participant must verify the official domain, official links, the smart contract address, and the source of the information.

9. Risk of Partner and Educational Materials

Information from partners, leaders, speakers, bloggers, chats, or third-party participants may be incomplete, outdated, or incorrect.

It is prohibited to treat anyone’s explanations as a guarantee of a result. If a partner promises guaranteed income, the absence of risk, fixed profit, a return on investment, or a mandatory payout, such wording is not the official position of Atlas.

The official Smart Cycle rules, the risk disclosure, the terms of use of the interface, and the current parameters of the smart contract take priority over verbal explanations.

10. Regulatory Uncertainty Risk

Web3, crypto assets, smart contracts, referral mechanics, and digital mutual-assistance systems may be regulated differently in different countries.

Rules may change. In certain jurisdictions, the use of such systems may be restricted, may require independent tax assessment, or may have legal consequences for the participant.

The participant is solely responsible for compliance with the laws of their country, the tax consequences, and their eligibility to participate.

11. Risk of Changes to the Interface and Parameters

The Atlas interface, reference materials, status displays, available functions, cycle parameters, fees, limits, and the language of descriptions may be updated.

Some changes may be related to technical updates, security, DAO decisions, the version of the smart contract, network conditions, or product development.

Before confirming an action, the participant must verify the current parameters in the interface, the wallet, and the published rules.

12. Service Availability Risk

The Atlas interface, website, API, analytics, FAQ, support, webinars, or other auxiliary services may be temporarily unavailable due to technical maintenance, overload, errors, blocks, attacks, hosting problems, network problems, or other reasons.

The unavailability of the interface does not always mean the smart contract is unavailable, but it may make interaction with it more difficult.

13. Tax and Personal Consequences

Any actions with crypto assets may have tax, accounting, or other personal consequences in the participant’s country.

Atlas does not calculate the participant’s taxes, does not file reports on the participant’s behalf, and does not provide individual tax advice.

14. Acceptance of Risk

By connecting a wallet, creating a Smart Cycle, submitting an assistance request, or using the Atlas interface, the participant confirms that:

they have read and understood this risk disclosure;

they understand the absence of guarantees;

they act voluntarily;

they accept the risk of loss of funds, delays, technical errors, and the absence of the expected result;

they bear sole responsibility for their decisions, wallet, device, network, and compliance with the laws of their country.

If the participant does not agree with the stated risks, they must not use the Atlas System.

Структура управления, вдохновлённая DAO Atlas System

1. Цель документа

Этот документ описывает подход Atlas к управлению: Atlas позиционируется не как полная DAO, но может использовать отдельные механики, вдохновленные DAO, для голосования, участия сообщества и обсуждения развития экосистемы.

2. Почему не “полностью DAO”

Atlas не должен обещать полную децентрализацию управления, если:

ключевые параметры смарт-контракта не полностью управляются децентрализованной автономной организацией;

команда сохраняет роль в разработке интерфейса, безопасности и материалов;

Не все решения выносятся на голосование;

механизмы управления вводятся постепенно;

юридическая, техническая или часть, касающаяся безопасности, остается за ответственными членами команды.

3. Какие механики, вдохновленные DAO, могут быть охвачены

Механики, вдохновленные DAO, могут использоваться для:

голосование по образовательным инициативам;

выбор тем для вебинаров;

оценка предложений сообщества;

приоритизация дорожной карты;

обсуждение улучшений интерфейса;

опросы сообщества;

предложения по безопасности;

отзывы о материалах;

Участие активных статусов в развитии экосистемы.

4. Что может решить управляющий орган сообщества

Общественное управление может обсуждать и голосовать по:

изменения интерфейса;

приоритеты дорожной карты;

образовательные инициативы;

Правила вебинара;

параметры общедоступных материалов;

предложения по безопасности;

разработка партнерской программы, при условии, что это не нарушит уже действующие правила.

5. Чего не должно делать управление

Управление не должно:

обеспечивать выплаты;

отменить действие Уведомления о рисках;

заставить участников действовать;

изменять закрытые ключи или учетные данные для доступа;

гарантировать ликвидность;

заменить смарт-контракт ручным решением.

6. Кто может участвовать

Право участвовать в определенных голосованиях может зависеть от:

подключенный кошелек;

статус участника;

активность в системе;

история участия;

роль в сообществе;

правила конкретного голосования.

7. Вес голоса

Вес голоса — это вес голоса участника в конкретном голосовании. Он может быть одинаковым для всех или зависеть от статуса, активности, репутации, токенов управления, NFT, срока участия или других опубликованных критериев.

Если используется вес голоса, это должно быть описано заранее, до начала голосования.

8. Жизненный цикл предложения

Стандартный жизненный цикл предложения может включать:

Проект идеи или предложения.

Предварительное обсуждение.

Публикация окончательного текста.

Период голосования.

Подсчет результата.

Публикация решения.

Исполнение, если решение относится к области, которую управление фактически может изменить.

9. Кворум и сроки

Голосование считается информационным, если кворум не достигнут. Если кворум достигнут, решение может быть принято к рассмотрению командой или исполнено в ранее описанных рамках управления.

10. Информационные и обязательные голоса

Не все голоса должны иметь одинаковую силу.

Атлас может иметь:

информационные общественные опросы;

консультативные голоса, которые помогают команде принять решение;

обязательные голоса, если определенная область была заранее делегирована механизму управления;

тестировать голоса на ранней стадии.

11. Связь с партнерской программой и вебинарами

Механизмы, основанные на DAO, могут быть использованы для выбора тем международных вебинаров, оценки докладчиков и сбора предложений по материалам, языкам, расписанию и инициативам сообщества.

Это не означает, что участники могут путем голосования переназначать рефералов других лиц, изменять привязку, отменять правила партнерской программы или присваивать структуру другого лица.

12. Строгие ограничения

